\frametitle{Explanation: one operator breaks a published algorithm}\framesubtitle {\footnotesize A one-character change --- not an algorithm change.}
\vspace{0.3em}
\begin{columns}[T]
\begin{column}{0.46\textwidth}
\begin{lstlisting}[basicstyle=\ttfamily\small,linewidth=\linewidth,xleftmargin=2pt]
#ifdef RIEMANN_STRICT_INEQUALITY
  if (SL <  0 && 0 <  S_star)   // strict
#else
  if (SL <= 0 && 0 <= S_star)   // baseline
#endif
\end{lstlisting}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.52\textwidth}
{\footnotesize
\begin{itemize}
\item Toro-2 by symmetry: $N_\ast=0$ exactly and $D_\ast=-2\rho a\neq 0$, so
      $S_\ast=0$ (a wave \emph{speed} of zero --- the contact is at rest).
\item Baseline $\le$: the test $S_L<0\le S_\ast$ holds at $S_\ast=0$, so it picks
      the star flux $F_{\ast L}$ $\to$ \textbf{zero} mass flux $\rho u$ across the
      face (correct). Run completes ($\approx 0.13$\,s).
\item Strict $<$: at $S_\ast=0$ the test $0<S_\ast$ is false, so neither star
      branch is taken; it falls through to $F_R$, which \textbf{injects} mass flux
      $\to$ negative density on step~0 $\to$ run fails.
\end{itemize}}
\end{column}
\end{columns}
\vspace{0.2em}
\centering{\footnotesize $S_\ast$ is a wave speed (here $0$); the difference is
whether the equality case is counted as the contact.}
\note{
This is the clearest example. Toro test two is a symmetric near-vacuum problem. By
symmetry, the top of the contact-speed formula cancels exactly and is zero, while
the bottom stays away from zero, so in exact arithmetic the speed is zero. But the
code computes this zero with rounding noise, so its sign depends on the noise. The
standard version uses a less-than-or-equal test, picks a valid flux, and finishes
in about a tenth of a second. If I change that one operator to a strict less-than,
the equal case takes the wrong flux: the density becomes negative on the very first
step, and the run fails. The key point is the mass flux across the face: the
baseline star branch gives exactly zero mass flux, which is correct for a contact
at rest, whereas the strict version falls through to the right flux and injects
mass flux, which drives the density negative. So a single character -- still the
same published algorithm -- is the difference between a clean run and no result at
all.
}
